1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,944
This session teaches all five saved sections of the school APGAR
module as topic-matched support for the explicit newborn practical. It
is not an invented school allocation.

2
00:00:10,944 --> 00:00:20,616
The skill is a consistent timed description and useful communication.
The score is not a replacement for immediate assessment of breathing
and heart rate.

3
00:00:20,616 --> 00:00:29,544
We will learn the components before interpreting the total. Knowing
what produced a number is more useful than reciting only eight out of
ten.

4
00:00:29,544 --> 00:00:40,296
The examples are original scoring exercises and not answers to the
school quiz. Actual neonatal resuscitation decisions belong to the
separate current algorithm session.

5
00:00:40,296 --> 00:00:51,600
Virginia Apgar developed the system in the early nineteen-fifties and
published it in nineteen fifty-three. The later acronym is a memory
aid, not five separate diagnoses.

6
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:59,112
Its strength is a common vocabulary for describing a newborn at a
specified time and reporting a change after support.

7
00:00:59,112 --> 00:01:10,944
The five observations include color, heart rate, reflex response, tone
and breathing effort. Some are subjective and all must be related to
the real clinical context.

8
00:01:10,944 --> 00:01:24,072
A historical origin does not mean every old treatment recommendation
remains current. Scoring principles and an old resuscitation algorithm
must not be treated as one inseparable document.

9
00:01:24,072 --> 00:01:36,072
The school uses triage-tag imagery for the score ranges, but that can
be misleading if interpreted literally. Treatment must not wait for
the total or be selected solely from its color band.

10
00:01:36,072 --> 00:01:45,864
A newborn who is apneic needs immediate appropriate care before the
one-minute score is due. A total alone cannot decide whether
compressions are indicated.

11
00:01:45,864 --> 00:01:55,704
The same total can come from different component combinations and
different support. State the observations and use the current newborn
pathway for action.

12
00:01:55,704 --> 00:02:06,648
A reassuring-looking total does not override poor breathing, a new
clinical change or an unreliable measurement. Continue direct
assessment and reassessment.

13
00:02:06,648 --> 00:02:18,024
Record the complete-birth time and use it as the timing reference.
Time of calling dispatch, head delivery or cord cutting is not
automatically the baby's age clock.

14
00:02:18,024 --> 00:02:30,288
Assign the one- and five-minute observations at the appropriate times
while necessary care continues. Do not wait until stabilization and
then label the later condition as the earlier score.

15
00:02:30,288 --> 00:02:41,568
When a precise scheduled observation was missed, document the actual
time and available information honestly. Do not invent a retrospective
normal score to fill a form.

16
00:02:41,568 --> 00:02:50,088
Use a team member for timing and documentation when resources permit.
That allows the treatment provider to continue effective support.

17
00:02:50,088 --> 00:03:04,848
The school introduction says seven or less, while its glossary says
less than seven. The selected AAP statement and the textbook support
less than seven at five minutes for repeated five-minute scoring up to
twenty minutes.

18
00:03:04,848 --> 00:03:15,912
A scheduled scoring interval is not permission to reassess the patient
only every five minutes. Breathing and heart-rate decisions remain
continuous and time-sensitive.

19
00:03:15,912 --> 00:03:24,888
Reaching seven does not automatically end all clinical monitoring, and
reaching twenty minutes does not independently direct cessation of
treatment.

20
00:03:24,888 --> 00:03:37,008
Do not use the old ten-minute-zero discussion as a field termination
rule. Current resuscitation decisions need the actual guideline,
consultation, clinical context and family communication.

21
00:03:37,008 --> 00:03:48,744
Each component receives zero, one or two points, and the total ranges
from zero to ten. Keep the component order consistent so nothing is
omitted or counted twice.

22
00:03:48,744 --> 00:03:59,760
Use observed findings at the time point. A treatment plan, a likely
future improvement or the fact that someone is ventilating does not
supply a spontaneous normal finding.

23
00:03:59,760 --> 00:04:08,640
Separate observation from interpretation. Record the actual rate or
response as well as the component score when useful for the handover.

24
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:18,312
If a component cannot be assessed reliably, use the applicable
documentation process and disclose the limitation rather than guess a
flattering total.

25
00:04:18,312 --> 00:04:29,616
The conventional appearance chart gives zero for an entirely blue or
pale appearance, one for central color with peripheral cyanosis and
two for the chart's entirely pink description.

26
00:04:29,616 --> 00:04:41,520
Do not interpret natural pigmentation as cyanosis or subtract points
because a baby does not literally have pale pink skin. Assess
appropriate central sites and document uncertainty.

27
00:04:41,520 --> 00:04:54,024
Acrocyanosis affects hands and feet and can occur during transition;
persistent central cyanosis or pallor has a different meaning. Neither
should be interpreted from skin color alone in poor lighting.

28
00:04:54,024 --> 00:05:04,968
Use reliable oxygenation and the clinical assessment for treatment
decisions. Do not invent a race-based numerical correction or let the
appearance score replace monitoring.

29
00:05:04,968 --> 00:05:14,424
The school's detailed criterion gives zero when no heartbeat is
detected, one for a present rate below one hundred, and two for one
hundred or more.

30
00:05:14,424 --> 00:05:23,808
That avoids the old textbook table's unassigned exactly-one-hundred
boundary. It does not mean a pulse of one hundred alone proves a
healthy transition.

31
00:05:23,808 --> 00:05:33,696
Use a reliable method and communicate the measured rate. Do not spend
a full minute delaying necessary support solely to resolve an urgent
rate estimate.

32
00:05:33,696 --> 00:05:43,752
Apgar pulse points are not compression thresholds. The current newborn
algorithm includes effectiveness and duration of ventilation before
its compression decision.

33
00:05:43,752 --> 00:05:54,264
Assess reflex irritability through an appropriate gentle stimulus such
as necessary drying or rubbing the back. Observe what the baby
actually does.

34
00:05:54,264 --> 00:06:04,056
No response scores zero, a grimace or weak response one, and a
vigorous response such as a strong cry, cough or sneeze two under the
school chart.

35
00:06:04,056 --> 00:06:16,152
Do not perform unnecessary deep suction, slap, shake or repeatedly
flick the baby to elicit a higher score. The assessment must not
create injury or delay ventilation.

36
00:06:16,152 --> 00:06:26,544
Describe the stimulus and response when useful. A reflex score alone
does not diagnose neurological damage or explain why the response is
reduced.

37
00:06:26,544 --> 00:06:36,048
Observe posture and spontaneous movement. Limpness, some flexion and
active movement with good flexion represent different component
findings.

38
00:06:36,048 --> 00:06:45,696
Assign zero for limpness, one for some flexion or limited activity and
two for active movement and good flexion according to the school
criteria.

39
00:06:45,696 --> 00:06:58,344
Prematurity, medication exposure, illness and injury can affect tone.
Do not equate a low tone point with a single diagnosis or ignore it
simply because the infant is preterm.

40
00:06:58,344 --> 00:07:07,872
Note asymmetry or a new change as a separate clinical finding. Do not
force painful limb extension merely to prove resistance for the score.

41
00:07:07,872 --> 00:07:21,744
Observe spontaneous breathing effort and listen for the cry. Zero
indicates absent breathing, one slow or irregular effort or weak cry,
and two good effort with a strong cry in the school description.

42
00:07:21,744 --> 00:07:30,816
A chest rise produced by positive-pressure ventilation is not
automatically evidence of spontaneous respiration worth two points.

43
00:07:30,816 --> 00:07:42,312
Do not interrupt necessary effective ventilation merely to obtain an
unassisted score. Use concurrent observations and the applicable
assisted-documentation process.

44
00:07:42,312 --> 00:07:53,280
Regardless of the points, inadequate breathing needs the current
newborn pathway now. Do not wait for the total or keep stimulating a
baby whose ventilation is failing.

45
00:07:53,280 --> 00:08:01,896
The school section telling you to stop ventilation to assess breathing
should not be treated as a routine instruction to interrupt life-
saving support.

46
00:08:01,896 --> 00:08:10,176
Document the support actually occurring at the time point along with
observations and component scores, using the approved reporting
format.

47
00:08:10,176 --> 00:08:22,032
A score obtained during assisted breathing is not equivalent to one in
a spontaneously breathing infant. Do not silently compare totals as
though the circumstances were identical.

48
00:08:22,032 --> 00:08:32,568
If information is unavailable, disclose it and record what is known.
Do not manufacture a complete perfect score or interrupt necessary
support to remove uncertainty.

49
00:08:32,568 --> 00:08:42,120
Use a consistent order and a clear time reference. Two people
assessing different moments may be describing real change rather than
one being wrong.

50
00:08:42,120 --> 00:08:54,192
When scores differ, compare specific component observations and the
support underway. A statement such as the total looks too low is not a
reason to alter documented findings.

51
00:08:54,192 --> 00:09:04,848
Acknowledge subjective elements and improve light, technique or
information when feasible. Do not use confidence or seniority as a
substitute for evidence.

52
00:09:04,848 --> 00:09:15,144
Continue effective care while discrepancies are clarified. The purpose
is reliable communication, not winning a scoring argument during
resuscitation.

53
00:09:15,144 --> 00:09:26,352
The school describes seven to ten, four to six and zero to three. The
selected professional statement places these descriptors in the five-
minute term and late-preterm context.

54
00:09:26,352 --> 00:09:39,312
These ranges summarize condition but do not select a universal package
of suction, oxygen or compressions. The underlying observations and
current algorithm determine what is needed.

55
00:09:39,312 --> 00:09:50,352
A high total does not prove every component is normal, and a low total
does not prove asphyxia. State the time, components, support and
clinical concern.

56
00:09:50,352 --> 00:09:59,088
Do not guarantee improvement by five minutes or cease observation
because a target score was reached. Reassess the actual trajectory.

57
00:09:59,088 --> 00:10:08,808
A preterm infant may score differently because of immaturity. That
does not prove asphyxia or mean that current inadequate breathing
needs no treatment.

58
00:10:08,808 --> 00:10:19,728
Maternal medicines and several illnesses can alter tone,
responsiveness or breathing. The score records the condition but
cannot identify the cause alone.

59
00:10:19,728 --> 00:10:29,400
Observer differences, lighting and treatment can change apparent
findings. Interpret a trend with the circumstances, not only the
arithmetic.

60
00:10:29,400 --> 00:10:41,472
Avoid racial or clinical bias by assessing and documenting actual
components. No appearance adjustment should substitute for an
appropriate objective oxygenation and clinical assessment.

61
00:10:41,472 --> 00:10:51,552
Lower scores can be associated with worse outcomes across populations.
An association does not let you predict the future neurological
function of one infant.

62
00:10:51,552 --> 00:11:03,960
A low score is nonspecific and cannot independently establish birth
asphyxia. More clinical and laboratory information is needed to assess
impaired gas exchange and its cause.

63
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:14,160
Do not tell families that a low score guarantees disability or a
higher score guarantees no injury. Explain what is being observed and
what support is underway.

64
00:11:14,160 --> 00:11:24,864
Use current consultation and team decision processes for serious
resuscitation questions. The number must not become a standalone
decision to withhold or stop care.

65
00:11:24,864 --> 00:11:37,416
This original scoring example has central color with blue hands and
feet, heart rate ninety, a weak grimace, some limb flexion and weak
irregular spontaneous breathing at one minute.

66
00:11:37,416 --> 00:11:46,008
Each observation earns one point in the school chart, so the total is
five. State all components rather than only the total.

67
00:11:46,008 --> 00:11:56,664
Necessary support is already underway according to the newborn
pathway. We did not wait to calculate five before responding to the
breathing and heart-rate findings.

68
00:11:56,664 --> 00:12:07,728
Record the time and concurrent support, and continue reassessment.
This one-minute total does not determine a prognosis or automatically
select chest compressions.

69
00:12:07,728 --> 00:12:20,424
Now assume a measured rate of one hundred thirty-six, vigorous
response, active flexion and a strong spontaneous cry at five minutes,
with blue hands and feet still present.

70
00:12:20,424 --> 00:12:29,352
The score is one plus two plus two plus two plus two, totaling nine.
Do not increase appearance to two merely to produce ten.

71
00:12:29,352 --> 00:12:41,064
Report how support and observations changed between time points. If
support remains, document it; an improving total does not erase the
care required to obtain that condition.

72
00:12:41,064 --> 00:12:51,288
Continue appropriate newborn monitoring and warmth. A total of nine is
not an independent discharge, transport-refusal or long-term-outcome
clearance.

73
00:12:51,288 --> 00:13:02,496
Start with complete-birth time, estimated gestation and the infant's
current age. State when each score was assigned and whether timing was
exact or estimated.

74
00:13:02,496 --> 00:13:13,512
Give the total with relevant component details and measured heart
rate. Explain limitations such as uncertain color or unavailable
spontaneous observations during support.

75
00:13:13,512 --> 00:13:25,584
Report ventilation, oxygen, airway support, compressions or medicines
actually given, with time and response. Do not imply unsupported
recovery when the infant remains assisted.

76
00:13:25,584 --> 00:13:33,768
Finish with the current clinical concern and transfer needs. The score
supports the story; it is not the entire handover.

77
00:13:33,768 --> 00:13:42,264
You should now describe each component and calculate an original
example without changing observations to fit a desired total.

78
00:13:42,264 --> 00:13:52,848
Use the actual birth clock and document time-specific support. Resolve
the school less-than-seven inconsistency without turning a scoring
interval into a treatment delay.

79
00:13:52,848 --> 00:14:02,952
Never stop necessary ventilation merely to obtain a pure score.
Current breathing and heart-rate findings guide care independently of
the arithmetic.

80
00:14:02,952 --> 00:14:13,104
Keep the limits visible when communicating with clinicians and
families. Accurate modest interpretation is more useful than a
confident unsupported prediction.
